<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Niger Coast Protectorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1893 CE – 1900 CE · capital Calabar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate in south-eastern Nigeria from 1893 to 1900, whose forces sacked Benin City in 1897.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1893 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oil-rivers-protectorate">Oil Rivers Protectorate</a> was extended inland and renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> Coast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>, under the commissioner and consul-general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-maxwell-macdonald">Claude MacDonald</a>, with headquarters at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-calabar">Old Calabar</a>. It was governed by the Foreign Office rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a>, and its officials began pushing into the hinterland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> built customs posts, courts and a constabulary, and used force against rulers who obstructed trade. In 1894 it defeated the Itsekiri trader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nana-olomu">Nana Olomu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebrohimi">Ebrohimi</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-moor">Ralph Moor</a>, commissioner from 1896, an expedition in 1897 avenged the killing of acting consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-robert-phillips">James Phillips</a> by sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>, ending the independence of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a> and seizing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 1 January 1900 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was merged with the southern lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> to form the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> taken in 1897 remain the subject of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a> campaigns.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/niger-coast-protectorate.jpg" alt="Niger Coast Protectorate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Niger_Coast_Protectorate_(1893%E2%80%931899).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1893 CE – 1900 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Calabar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Claude MacDonald, Ralph Moor</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Efik, Ijaw, Edo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1896&amp;focus=niger-coast-protectorate" aria-label="Niger Coast Protectorate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4855 1987 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Niger Coast Protectorate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5175 2243L5155 2197M5175 2243L5175 2243M5175 2243L4997 651M5175 2243L5094 2192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5155 2197h0M5175 2243h0M4997 651h0M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5175 2243h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5175 2243h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1896&amp;focus=niger-coast-protectorate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
